This is a fantastic edge case to run into, and it perfectly highlights why distributed systems are tricky.
Here is exactly what happened:
 1. When you clicked "Approve", the system started the HDFS_SYNC_START phase.
 2. Because this is the *very first time* your application tried to talk to the Data Lake since Spring Boot started, your **Hikari Connection Pool (HikariPool-2) tried to initialize and open a socket to the Spark Thrift Server.**
 3. **The Thrift Server is currently unreachable** (either it is offline, or your application.properties URL is wrong).
 4. Because it couldn't connect, Hikari threw a fatal PoolInitializationException.
### The Bug (Why it failed the batch)
If you remember our fallback logic, we wrapped the HDFS sync in a try-catch block designed to catch DataAccessException so it could quietly queue the failure and leave the batch as ACCEPTED.
However, PoolInitializationException is a raw Hikari exception—it is **not** a Spring DataAccessException. It slipped right past our safety net, crashed the thread, and triggered the global Exception catch block at the bottom, which immediately marked your batch as ERROR!
### The Fix
We need to broaden the catch block in your JournalRequestServiceImpl to intercept *any* exception thrown by the Hive connection so that network/pool failures don't ruin a successful Oracle database approval.
**File:** com/fincore/JournalService/Service/JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
**Find this block inside executeApprovalInTransaction (around line 793):**
```java
            // HDFS sync is fire-and-forget; failure queues for retry
            try {
                hdfsSyncService.syncToDataLake(syncData);
            } catch (DataAccessException hdfsEx) {
                log.error("HDFS_SYNC_FAIL | Batch: {} | Status: Background recovery queued", batchId);
                jdbcTemplate.update("INSERT INTO HDFS_SYNC_RETRY_QUEUE (BATCH_ID, STATUS) VALUES (?, 'PENDING')", batchId);
            }

```
**Replace it entirely with this:**
```java
            // HDFS sync is fire-and-forget; failure queues for retry
            try {
                hdfsSyncService.syncToDataLake(syncData);
            } catch (Exception hdfsEx) { 
                // FIX: Catch ALL exceptions (including Hikari PoolInitializationException)
                log.error("HDFS_SYNC_FAIL | Batch: {} | Error: {} | Status: Background recovery queued", batchId, hdfsEx.getClass().getSimpleName());
                jdbcTemplate.update("INSERT INTO HDFS_SYNC_RETRY_QUEUE (BATCH_ID, STATUS) VALUES (?, 'PENDING')", batchId);
            }

```
### Next Steps
Once you make this change and restart:
 1. If you approve the batch again, it will successfully mark it as **ACCEPTED** in Oracle.
 2. The HikariPool will still fail to initialize, but this time it will be caught, and the batch ID will be safely inserted into HDFS_SYNC_RETRY_QUEUE.
**However, you still have an infrastructure problem:** Your Spring Boot app cannot reach the Thrift server.
 * Double-check your application.properties. If your URL says jdbc:hive2://spark-thrift:10000, and you are running the app locally on your laptop, your laptop doesn't know what spark-thrift is. You must change it to the actual IP address of the Kubernetes node/load balancer, or map it in your hosts file.
